{"uid":"50154c9f76c1046e","name":"test_loop_subprocess_dispatch_sites_are_owned_by_exactly_these_functions","fullName":"tests.test_tier_dispatch#test_loop_subprocess_dispatch_sites_are_owned_by_exactly_these_functions","historyId":"97a6acd4aa1ba31dd8962b094f6a8e7d","time":{"start":1791921765342,"stop":1791921765349,"duration":7},"description":"Task 002 (steer 002 item 2): `runner.run(...)` -- the actual\n    subprocess launch -- appears in loop.py ONLY inside the three named\n    owning functions. The old assertion here was a bare `len(hits) == 2`,\n    which only proved a *count*: a 4th call site inside some brand-new\n    function would have kept the count wrong (3 != 2) but a *swap* --\n    deleting one of the two known sites while adding two new ones\n    elsewhere -- would have sailed through unnoticed, and simply bumping\n    the literal to `== 3` (the naive fix for `_ask_contract_fix` landing)\n    would reopen exactly that hole for a would-be 4th site. Asserting the\n    SET of owning function names instead pins identity, not just\n    cardinality: any unlisted owner -- a stray new call site, or a call\n    site moved out of its named function -- fails by naming the offender.\n    ","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 002 (steer 002 item 2): <code>runner.run(...)</code> -- the actual<br />\nsubprocess launch -- appears in loop.py ONLY inside the three named<br />\nowning functions. The old assertion here was a bare <code>len(hits) == 2</code>,<br />\nwhich only proved a <em>count</em>: a 4th call site inside some brand-new<br />\nfunction would have kept the count wrong (3 != 2) but a <em>swap</em> --<br />\ndeleting one of the two known sites while adding two new ones<br />\nelsewhere -- would have sailed through unnoticed, and simply bumping<br />\nthe literal to <code>== 3</code> (the naive fix for <code>_ask_contract_fix</code> landing)<br />\nwould reopen exactly that hole for a would-be 4th site. Asserting the<br />\nSET of owning function names instead pins id